  "note": "Follow-up to the 3.9 condition check after the public-set reruns (Phase D). The false alarms behind it (\"if necessary\", an anaphoric \"If not,\", sources with no space between sentences) were first observed on the public sets (RAGTruth); the fix was written against new dev cases, not cases from those sets (__tests__/unit/evaluate/condition-consistency.test.js), and the public sets were rerun once after it. Legal boilerplate (\"except as required by law\" and similar) was made non-restricting by a maintainer decision taken after the condition-dropped test split showed 4 of 4 false alarms on it, so the test-split rows after the fix are not a held-out measure; all of their change is those four cases. Known causes of the remaining public-set false alarms, left alone because they were found on those sets: a clause cut at an abbreviation (\"if the U.S.\" read as \"if the U\") and structured sources (JSON records with review text) read as one sentence. Every judge call was a cache hit (no new API spend). Then (2026-11-07) the bug-hunt fixes (text normalisation and sentence splitting, BH-1 to BH-6) and a fix for full stops inside abbreviations in restricting clauses (\"if the U.S.\"; written against new dev cases, __tests__/unit/evaluate/condition-abbreviations.test.js) were measured once each: lexical, SciFact and condition-dropped rows from the cache with no misses, so exact; of the judges only Claude Haiku 5.5 was rerun (RAGTruth sample, full test split and HaluEval; the full split and HaluEval judge rows were not run on 3.9 before), after both fixes; the other judge rows were not rerun on the bug-hunt fixes. The condition-dropped judge rows for these two stages were all cache hits, so they are exact too. With the BH-15 fix, a judge reply with no text (cut off at maxTokens) is a judge error and the lexical verdict stands, where checkAnswer used to reject; the 3.8.0 run had no such reply, and at least one full-split answer on this rerun did.",
